# Crossbar in private mode: the tailnet is the way in, and the public hostname is not one.
#
# The counterpart to `crossbar-public.service`, and read the comments there first: the same
# check decides which of the two runs, the same `Before=` keeps the server from opening
# anything before the box is in shape, and the same reason applies for using commands rather
# than dependency directives to move Caddy.

[Unit]
Description=Crossbar in private mode (the tailnet in, the public hostname out)

After=network-online.target tailscaled.service
Wants=network-online.target tailscaled.service

Before=crossbar.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# `RemainAfterExit` is deliberately absent, and that absence is load-bearing. A oneshot
# without it goes inactive the moment it has run, which is the whole mechanism of a switch:
# the units are not active afterwards, so the next `systemctl restart crossbar` starts them
# again and the shape is re-applied every time. `RemainAfterExit=yes` would leave this one
# "active (exited)" after its first run; every later restart would find it already active,
# skip it, and leave the box in the previous mode while reporting a clean restart. If you
# are reading this because you are about to add it: don't.
EnvironmentFile=/home/admin/crossbar/.env

# Which mode this unit is for is answered by the same file the server reads, so there is one
# answer to "which mode is this deployment in" and no second copy to drift.
#
# `grep` here rather than `ConditionEnvironment=`, which looks like the obvious tool and is
# not: that condition is checked against the *manager's* environment (/proc/1/environ), so
# `EnvironmentFile=` never reaches it and the check fails in both modes. Measured -- both
# units were skipped, including the one whose mode was in force -- and a condition that
# fails quietly is worse than no condition, because the box looks switched and is not.
#
# `-x` is a whole-line match, so a comment or a longer name that merely contains this one
# cannot satisfy it.
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/grep -qx CROSSBAR_NETWORK_MODE=private /home/admin/crossbar/.env

# The public route goes first. Caddy is what holds port 443 on the public address, so
# stopping it is what makes "the tailnet is the way in" true rather than merely preferred --
# and it happens before the tailnet opens, so the two are never both available.
#
# Stopping a unit that is already stopped succeeds, so this is also the state it wants.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl stop caddy

# Then the server's loopback listener, published on the tailnet's own port 443 with a
# certificate for the node's name -- which is why private mode needs no certificate of its
# own and no public DNS record. The server stays on loopback in both modes; what the mode
# changes is which of the two front doors is open onto it.
#
# `${PORT:-3003}`, not `$PORT`: the port is the server's own listener, read from the same
# file, so the proxy target and the listener cannot disagree -- but a file with no `PORT`
# line must fall back rather than expand to nothing, which would leave `tailscale serve` an
# empty argument where the port belongs, with nothing reporting the mismatch. 3003 is the
# same fallback the server itself uses (`src/config.js`, `integer('PORT', 3003, …)`) and the
# one Caddy's `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$PORT:3003}` uses (`deploy/Caddyfile:76`), so all
# three agree on an unconfigured box. systemd expands `${NAME:-word}` against *this unit's*
# environment -- the one `EnvironmentFile=` above loads -- not the manager's, which is the
# opposite of the `ConditionEnvironment=` trap noted above and the reason this is safe here.
#
# `tailscaled` keeps this rather than this unit, so a reboot brings the address back on its
# own; this is here so that a switch reaches the tailnet's configuration too, which a reboot
# alone would not.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/tailscale serve --bg ${PORT:-3003}

# Then the relay, if this box runs one. Same reason and same shape as in
# `crossbar-public.service` (read the comment there): coturn renders its `realm` and
# `external-ip` from `.env` once, at its own start, so a relay kept across a switch goes on
# advertising the mode just left, and that presents as calls that will not relay.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/systemctl try-restart crossbar-turn.service
